.he "init (1)                     3/18/82                     init (1)" 
.fo "                              -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd      
PROGRAM
.in +5
.br
init


.in -5
.bd
USAGE          
.in +5
.br
.bd
/etc/init


.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION      
.in +5
.br
.bd
Init
creates an automatic process
for each terminal on a which a user may log in.

.bd
Init
first brings the system up in single-user mode, 
with only the console active.
If there is a password on the "root" account,
it demands this first. (Therefore, it is a good idea
to keep a Micronix diskette with an unprotected root
account, in case the root password is lost.)

When the single user shell exits, 
.bd
init
runs the shell script "/etc/rc" if it exists,
then reads the file "/etc/ttys", and branches several times
to create a process for each terminal specified in the file.

When one of init's child processes terminates,
a new process is generated to take its place.  Normally, this 
takes place automatically.

The format of the "/etc/tty" file is as follows,
(one per line):
.nf
.bd
		tty A (9600) (on) (shake)
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SIGNALS
.in +5
.br
.bd
Init
catches two signals and ignores every other one.

A hangup signal
.bd
SIGHUP
makes
.bd
init
read "/etc/ttys" again.  A terminal turned on since the file was last read
gets a process; a terminal turned off keeps the one it has until the user
logged in there logs out, and is not started again.  Editing "/etc/ttys" on
its own changes nothing.  The file is read when the system comes up and
when this signal arrives, and at no other time.

A terminate signal
.bd
SIGTERM
takes the system down.
.bd
Init
kills every process and waits for it to die, logs out the user it belonged
to, unmounts each filesystem named in "/etc/mtab", writes a message on the
console and then stops.  The
.bd
down
command is this signal sent to process 1, and is the way to use it.

Both signals are turned off while
.bd
init
is acting on either of them, so a hangup arriving in the middle of a
re-read cannot start it over on a half-built table.  A signal sent while
they are off is lost, not held.

The shell's
.bd
kill
sends signal 9 and takes no signal number, and 9 cannot be caught, so
neither of these can be sent with it.
.bd
Down
is the only way to signal
.bd
init
from a terminal.

None of this reaches a login shell.  A caught signal goes back to its
default when a child execs, and
.bd
init
sets the defaults back itself before it starts a terminal or runs
"/etc/rc".


.sp 2
